/* book Worked example: Q: A multiply-accumulate datapath has tmult = 3.0 ns and tadd = 1.6 ns. Every register has tcq = 0.2 ns and tsu = 0.1 ns. Find the clock period, clock frequency and latency (a) without pipelining, (b) with one register after the multiplier, (c) with the multiplier also split into two 1.5 ns halves. (d) How long does design (c) take for 1000 samples, against design (a)?
   1. (a) T = 0.2 + 3.0 + 1.6 + 0.1 = 4.9 ns, so f = 1 ÷ 4.9 ns = 204.1 MHz. Latency = 4.9 ns.
   2. (b) Stages are 3.0 ns and 1.6 ns. T = 0.2 + 3.0 + 0.1 = 3.3 ns, so f = 303.0 MHz. Latency = 2 × 3.3 = 6.6 ns. Speed-up = 4.9 ÷ 3.3 = 1.48, far from 2, because the stages are unbalanced.
   3. (c) Stages are 1.5, 1.5 and 1.6 ns. T = 0.2 + 1.6 + 0.1 = 1.9 ns, so f = 526.3 MHz. Latency = 3 × 1.9 = 5.7 ns. Speed-up = 4.9 ÷ 1.9 = 2.58, not 3, because each stage pays 0.3 ns of overhead and the adder stage is 0.1 ns slower than the others.
   4. (d) Design (a): 1000 × 4.9 = 4900 ns. Design (c): (k + N − 1) × T = (3 + 999) × 1.9 = 1002 × 1.9 = 1903.8 ns, about 2.57 times faster.
   Answer: Unpipelined: 4.9 ns, 204.1 MHz, latency 4.9 ns. Two stages: 3.3 ns, 303.0 MHz, latency 6.6 ns. Three stages: 1.9 ns, 526.3 MHz, latency 5.7 ns; 1000 samples take 1903.8 ns instead of 4900 ns. */
