/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Each class needs its own interface.
   - Clock skew δ is the difference in arrival time of the same clock edge at two registers: δ = arrival at the capturing register − arrival at the launching register.
   - T + δ ≥ tcq + tlogic + tsu + 2tjit
   - Here the setup check: T is the clock period and tlogic the longest logic delay.
   - tcq,cd + tlogic,cd ≥ th + δ + 2tjit
   - Here the hold check: tcq,cd and tlogic,cd are the contamination (fastest) delays of the register and the shortest logic path.
   - So positive skew helps setup and hurts hold; negative skew (clock against the data) helps hold and costs speed; jitter only hurts.
   - A self-timed circuit replaces the global clock with local handshakes.
   - The receiver must know when the logic has finished.
   - The Muller C-element joins handshakes.
*/
