/* book Worked example: Q: A register-to-register path has tcq = 0.25 ns, tlogic = 1.8 ns (longest), tsu = 0.10 ns, th = 0.08 ns, contamination delays tcq,cd = 0.15 ns and tlogic,cd = 0.20 ns, and jitter tjit = 0.05 ns. Find the shortest clock period and check hold for skew (a) δ = 0, (b) δ = +0.2 ns, (c) δ = −0.2 ns. Fix any hold failure.
   1. Fixed sums: tcq + tlogic + tsu + 2tjit = 0.25 + 1.8 + 0.10 + 0.10 = 2.25 ns. Short path: tcq,cd + tlogic,cd = 0.15 + 0.20 = 0.35 ns.
   2. (a) δ = 0: T ≥ 2.25 ns, fmax = 1 ÷ 2.25 ns = 444.4 MHz. Hold needs 0.35 ≥ 0.08 + 0 + 0.10 = 0.18 ns: passes with 0.17 ns to spare.
   3. (b) δ = +0.2 ns: T ≥ 2.25 − 0.2 = 2.05 ns, fmax = 487.8 MHz. Hold needs 0.35 ≥ 0.08 + 0.2 + 0.10 = 0.38 ns: fails by 0.03 ns.
   4. Fix for (b): add a buffer of at least 0.03 ns to the short path only (for example 0.05 ns): 0.35 + 0.05 = 0.40 ≥ 0.38 ns. The long path, and so fmax, is unchanged.
   5. (c) δ = −0.2 ns: T ≥ 2.25 + 0.2 = 2.45 ns, fmax = 408.2 MHz. Hold needs 0.35 ≥ 0.08 − 0.2 + 0.10 = −0.02 ns: passes with 0.37 ns to spare.
   Answer: δ = 0: 2.25 ns (444.4 MHz), hold passes. δ = +0.2 ns: 2.05 ns (487.8 MHz) but hold fails by 0.03 ns until a 0.05 ns buffer is added to the short path. δ = −0.2 ns: 2.45 ns (408.2 MHz), hold passes easily. */
